{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.WenzlWeightLevelR","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.WenzlWeightLevelR","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.OcneanuWeightWenzl"],"declarations":15,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.levelBracket_two_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.levelBracket_two_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":214,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L214-L219","doc":"The level-2 bracket is nonzero exactly under the hypothesis the convention-D chain already\ncarries, so `wenzlWeight_eq_zero_iff` at `r = 2` needs nothing new. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlMarkovParam_conventionD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlMarkovParam_conventionD","kind":"theorem","line":203,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L203-L212","doc":"**The level-2 Markov parameter is `q · z_D`.**\n\nThe factor `q` is the Hecke-normalisation shift between `g² = (Q−1)g + Q` and `σ² = (q − q⁻¹)σ + 1`,\nunder which `σ = g/q` and `Q = q²` — the same shift `wenzlLevel_conventionD` inverts through. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlWeight_two_eq_ocneanuWeight","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlWeight_two_eq_ocneanuWeight","kind":"theorem","line":193,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L193-L201","doc":"**The level-2 weight is the convention-D Ocneanu weight**, on the nose.\n\n`wenzlWeight 2 (q²) μ = ocneanuWeight q (q³/(q²+1)) μ` — so every general-`r` statement above\nspecialises back to a statement about the weight the merged convention-D chain works with. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.level_unique_of_wenzlMarkovParam_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.level_unique_of_wenzlMarkovParam_eq","kind":"theorem","line":169,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L169-L189","doc":"**Equal level-`r` Markov parameters force equal levels.**\n\nThe `q` of `z_r = Q^r/(q·[r]_Q)` is the Hecke-normalisation shift; clearing it lands on Wenzl's\n`Q^r/[r]_Q`, where `level_unique_of_markovParameter` applies. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlMarkovParam_spec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlMarkovParam_spec","kind":"theorem","line":162,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L162-L167","doc":"Wenzl's parameter relation holds at `z_W = Q^r / [r]_Q`, in the denominator-cleared form\n`QBeta.WenzlWeight.level_unique_of_markovParameter` consumes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlMarkovParam_eq","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlMarkovParam_eq","kind":"theorem","line":148,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L148-L158","doc":"**Wenzl's level-`r` Markov parameter, in bracket form.**\n\n`z_W = Q^r(1−Q)/(1−Q^r) = Q^r / [r]_Q`.\n\nThe `(1−Q)/(1−Q^r)` of the literature is `1/[r]_Q` once the geometric factor is cancelled; the\nbracket form is the one that survives `Q = 1`, and the one the normalisation of `wenzlWeight` is\nstated in. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlWeight_ne_zero_of_le_rows","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlWeight_ne_zero_of_le_rows","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L139-L144","doc":"The surviving side, stated positively: at most `r` rows ⇒ nonzero weight. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlWeight_eq_zero_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlWeight_eq_zero_iff","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L121-L137","doc":"**The vanishing criterion at a general level.**\n\n`w_μ^{(r)}(Q) = 0 ⟺ μ has more than `r` rows.`\n\nAt `r = 2` this is the Temperley–Lieb row cap; at general `r` it is the `SU(r)` level-`r` cap, and\nit is the reason the level-`r` trace factors through the quotient by the `≥ (r+1)`-row blocks.\n\nThe root-of-unity hypothesis `hQ` is doing real work — without it a *hook length* can kill a\ndenominator and the `iff` fails — and so is `hbr`: at `[r]_Q = 0` the normalisation is undefined and\nevery weight would read as `0/0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlWeight_bot","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlWeight_bot","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L118-L119","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlWeight","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.wenzlWeight","kind":"def","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L109-L116","doc":"**The normalised Wenzl weight at level `r`.**\n\n`w_μ^{(r)}(Q) = s_μ(1, Q, …, Q^{r−1}) / [r]_Q^{|μ|}`.\n\nThe normalisation is the one that makes `∑_{μ ⊢ n} f^μ w_μ = tr(1) = 1`: each of the `n` cells\ncontributes one factor of `[r]_Q`, which is exactly what the branching identity consumes. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.levelBracket_ne_zero_of_gen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.levelBracket_ne_zero_of_gen","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L94-L105","doc":"**The level bracket is nonzero under genericity.**\n\n`[r]_Q = 0` would make `1 − Q^r = (1 − Q)·[r]_Q` vanish, i.e. `Q^r = 1` with `r ≠ 0`.  So the `hbr`\nside condition carried separately through the level-`r` chain is *implied* by `hgen`, and the chain\nruns on one genericity assumption rather than two. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.ne_one_of_gen","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.ne_one_of_gen","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L90-L92","doc":"A `Q` that is not a root of unity is not `1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.one_sub_pow_eq_mul_levelBracket","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.one_sub_pow_eq_mul_levelBracket","kind":"theorem","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L79-L88","doc":"`1 − Q^r = (1 − Q)·[r]_Q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.levelBracket_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.levelBracket_two","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L76-L77","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.levelBracket","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.levelBracket","kind":"def","line":70,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/WenzlWeightLevelR.lean#L70-L74","doc":"`[r]_Q = 1 + Q + ⋯ + Q^{r−1}`.\n\nThis is `s_{[1]}(1, Q, …, Q^{r−1})`, the principal specialisation of the one-box Schur function —\nthe factor Wenzl's normalisation divides by, once per cell. ","signatureChunk":0}]}